Yes. You’re separating several things that ordinary language collapses into “attraction,” “admiration,” or “jealousy.” They have different acquisition pathways.

The umbrella concept I’d use is acquisitive attraction: salience toward a trait because some part of you wants that trait incorporated into your lineage, behavior, identity, or environment.

Then split it by where the trait gets instantiated:

* Self-directed acquisitive attraction — “I want to become more like that.”
    You see strength, confidence, style, humor, competence, posture, status behavior, etc., and experience an impulse toward emulation. The acquisition channel is learning: observation → imitation → practice → internalization. This overlaps with prestige bias, social learning, identification, and mimesis.
* Mate-directed acquisitive attraction — “I want that trait joined with mine.”
    A physical or heritable characteristic becomes attractive partly because mating can propagate it into offspring. You don’t acquire their height, facial structure, pigmentation, etc.; the lineage potentially acquires a recombined version. Evolutionary biology would put much of this under intersexual selection / mate choice.

And there is a third category hiding inside your observation:

Competitive acquisitive attraction — “They possess something I want, and their possession exposes my lack of it.”

That’s where admiration and envy become strangely close. The nervous system detects a valuable trait before the conscious mind necessarily decides whether the bearer is a model, rival, mate, ally, or teacher. Then context determines the acquisition strategy.

So you get different transmission geometries:

Imitation
OTHER → observation → SELF
You acquire the pattern yourself.

Association / immersion
OTHER ↔ SELF → repeated exposure → SELF changes
Language, manners, norms, confidence, tastes, knowledge. Your “osmosis” metaphor fits cultural transmission, although obviously not literal biological osmosis.

Material transfer
OTHER → SELF
Food, microbiota, antibodies in certain contexts, transplanted tissue, information stored externally, etc. Something physically crosses the boundary.

Reproductive recombination
SELF + OTHER → CHILD
Neither parent gains the other’s trait. A third instantiation is generated containing recombined inherited information.

And that last one is conceptually unusual because it is acquisition without self-modification.

You wanted the thing.

You performed an operation.

But the resulting property appears in another entity.

That gives you a useful distinction between:

Assimilative acquisition

I acquire the trait.

and

Generative acquisition

I acquire the trait for something I create.

Sexual reproduction is therefore not merely transfer. It’s a kind of forward-instantiation operator:

A + B → C(A′, B′)

The desired information crosses generations rather than crossing directly between bodies.

And then culture gives us an even stranger version because it can travel in all directions. Your father can give you a gene, but your child cannot give you that gene retroactively. Yet your child can teach you slang tomorrow. A stranger can alter your behavior without touching you. Someone dead for 2,000 years can modify your cognition through a book.

So I’d actually organize the whole thing as an Inheritance Geometry:

genetic: vertical, generative
epigenetic/developmental: mostly vertical + environmental
behavioral: horizontal/oblique/vertical
cultural: effectively omnidirectional
material: direct physical transfer
reproductive: recombinational, instantiated downstream

Which produces the deeper principle you’re circling:

Wanting a trait does not specify how the trait can be acquired. The ontology of the trait determines the available inheritance channel.

A physique might require training.
A mannerism can be copied.
Knowledge can be taught.
Status can sometimes be conferred.
Genes require inheritance or biotechnology.
And some combinations can only exist by creating a new entity capable of embodying them.

That distinction between assimilation, transmission, and generation is much more powerful than treating all of them as simply “wanting what someone else has.”